A||||9||kiteman||Timing Erratic on '84||||10||
Z||000000||kiteman||03-11-2001||10:27 PM||||Just new to this board. Seems like lots of really good mechanics post to it.<P>I tried to set the timing for my '84.  Jumpered out terminals A&B in the ALCD, per instructions.  The timing was somewhat erratic - varied between 6 - 10 degrees when I set it to spec (8 deg.) Replaced plugs, wires, cap and rotor using AC-Delco before I set timing.  Is this variation normal, or do I have a problem?  Engine runs smoothly.<P>I used an inductive timing light on #1 Cylinder.||||reg||10
Z||000001||StuGood||03-12-2001||03:24 PM||||Don't you know engineers can't fix things?  We just tell other people how to build things wrong!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I don't believe variation in timing is normal.  If memory serves, the timing on my cars is rock-steady.  The only things I can think of involve pulling the distributor (checking for wear, replacing pickup coil while you're at it, etc.).  If you're concerned about it, I'd pick a weekend to do a thorough distributor rebuild.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Diamond Joe Quimby||03-13-2001||01:10 PM||||The Ogre posted a link to a memo about a diode that can go bad in the alternator that causes a low voltage ac signal to be fed back to the ECM, which causes erratic timing. I don't remember the link, but maybe you can do a search and find that thread.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kiteman||03-13-2001||07:58 PM||||Thanks to both of you. I'll look at changing the pickup coil (Don't have a spare weekend to do a distributor rebuild), and check out the lead on the diode in the alternator.<P>Incidentially I'm a chemical engineer, which means I'm totally unqualified to fix mechanical problems.  I appreciate all the help I can get! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000004||Bob Englert||03-13-2001||08:08 PM||||When I had my 85 SE 4 cyl I had the same problem with jumpy timing.  The car ran great, I just averaged out the indications to set it.  <P>It didn't seem to cause me any problems so I didn't try to track it down.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||03-13-2001||09:22 PM||||I rarely work on 4 cylinders, mainly V6's - however some things still apply here. I would check for side to side play from the distributor shaft, if the slightest - replace the unit, if problem still persisits, perhaps the Distributor is not tightened down or there may be excesive wear in the timing gear.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-14-2001||01:18 AM||||The distributor gear is probably a little worn.  I have two 4cylinder distributors and both of them behave the same way.<P>Try taking off the cap and see if you can turn the rotor.  I find that I can get a few degrees of axial play (it turns a little in each direction before the gear teeth come into contact).<P>The problem is that aside from a rebuilt distributor, I have not found a replacement distributor gear.  So, I just average the range of timing measurements at cylinder 1 and 4.  Then I take the average of the two.  It works out so that cylinder 4 is at an average of about 6 degrees and cylinder 1 is at an average of about 10 degrees.<P>After reading your post, I am surprised to find our experiences so similar.  I have a rebuilt alternator with &lt;1000miles on it, so I am having trouble believing it has a diode.  <P>I am just looking for a NEW distributor gear. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||kiteman||03-14-2001||08:05 PM||||Thanks again to all who took the time to post replys. This board is really great!!<P>As far as the causes are concerned, I think I can eliminate the distributor being not tight enough.  I put most of my feeble muscle power on the hold down nut with a flare wrench, so I don't think that dude is gonna' move anytime soon. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think the timing gear and chain are probably OK too.  The engine only has ~20K miles on it.  It was replaced (courtesy Pontiac)when I found out it was one of the blocks that had casting problems (used coolant).<P>I'll look for axial and/or lateral play in the distributor shaft.  If I find it, I'll probably go the route of replacing the distributor.<P>If I don't find a cause, at least I know from the posts that it's something I can live with without bad, bad things happening.<P>Again, thanks to all!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||c m west||03-14-2001||08:09 PM||||btw - just to let you know, the 4 cylinder uses a gear set, not a chain<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||kiteman||03-15-2001||07:29 PM||||Thanks.  I was looking at the Six cyl. in Chiltons which discussed the timing chain.  Assumed it applied to the four.  You know what they say about assume! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
